      "answer": "$\\dfrac{289}{6}$",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "Centre is $(3,-2)$. B1\nGradient of radius $CP=(4-(-2))/(5-3)=3$. *M1 Finding gradient using their centre (not $(0,0)$) and $P(5,4)$.\nEquation of tangent $y-4=-\\frac13(x-5)$. DM1 Using $P$ and the negative reciprocal of their radius gradient.\nIntercepts $x=17$ and $y=\\frac{17}{3}$. A1\nArea $=\\frac12\\times\\frac{17}{3}\\times17=\\frac{289}{6}$. A1; or $48\\frac16$, or 48.2 AWRT.\nAlternative method: $2x+2y\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-6+4\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0$. B1\nAt $P$, $10+8\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-6+4\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac13$. *M1 Find the gradient using $P(5,4)$ in their implicit differential, with at least one correctly differentiated $y$ term.\nThen $y-4=-\\frac13(x-5)$. DM1; intercepts $x=17$, $y=17/3$. A1; area $289/6$. A1.\nFurther alternative: $y=-2\\pm\\sqrt{40-(x-3)^2}$, leading to $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(3-x)(31+6x-x^2)^{-1/2}$. B1 OE; correct differentiation of the rearranged equation.\nAt $x=5$, $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(3-5)(31+6(5)-5^2)^{-1/2}=-\\frac13$. *M1 with clear chain rule. Then the same tangent (DM1), intercepts (A1) and area (A1).\nTotal: 5",
